  "case": {
    "presentation": "A 34-year-old man presents for follow-up after an emergency department visit for palpitations, tachycardia, arm numbness, and a sensation of tightness in the neck, thought to be due to stress/anxiety as he has been getting poor sleep due to international travel and jet lag. He had a negative troponin, normal TSH, and normal A1c, but his LDL was 200. What interventions should be done for this patient?",
    "recommendations": "For the chest pain, palpitations, and tachycardia, consider an EKG, echocardiogram, EKG stress test, and Ziopatch. If the baseline EKG is abnormal, convert the EKG stress test to a stress echocardiogram. Optional testing could include a CT coronary calcium score. For the elevated LDL in a patient < 40 years old, focus on lifestyle modification, including a heart-healthy diet. If that is not successful, initiate shared-decision making discussions about starting a moderate-intensity statin for primary prevention. If the patient has xanthomas or arcus cornealis or a family history of a first-degree relative with premature CAD or vascular disease, refer for workup for familial hypercholesterolemia."
